&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Free checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Teams recording tickets often show up the same week you tighten Conditional Access around guests and compliant devices — users feel "I can't record" and "externals can't open the link" as one outage. Before you enforce, the free &lt;strong&gt;Conditional Access pre-enforcement checklist&lt;/strong&gt; keeps break-glass accounts, pilot groups, and report-only review on one page:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;👉 &lt;a href="https://cashflow4375.gumroad.com/l/ujkqxb?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=aps_first_sale&amp;amp;utm_content=teams-meeting-recording-not-working" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://cashflow4375.gumroad.com/l/ujkqxb?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=aps_first_sale&amp;amp;utm_content=teams-meeting-recording-not-working&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want the rest of the M365 admin runway written down for the team (break/fix cards, offboarding and mailbox decisions, and read-only PowerShell snapshots), the &lt;strong&gt;Intune &amp;amp; M365 Admin Starter Pack&lt;/strong&gt; pairs with this triage: &lt;a href="https://cashflow4375.gumroad.com/l/joonf?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=aps_first_sale&amp;amp;utm_content=teams-meeting-recording-not-working" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://cashflow4375.gumroad.com/l/joonf?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=aps_first_sale&amp;amp;utm_content=teams-meeting-recording-not-working&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Admin Pack Studio; not affiliated with Microsoft. Steps 1–8 favor read-only checks: meeting type, Meeting options, Teams admin center / &lt;code&gt;Get-CsTeamsMeetingPolicy&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;Get-CsOnlineUser&lt;/code&gt;, OneDrive and SharePoint Recordings folders, Service Health, and Microsoft 365 admin diagnostics. Changing meeting/calling/events policies, meeting options, roles, expiration, sharing, and downloading async media are deliberate changes. Change one layer at a time, retest in a short meeting, and check current Microsoft Learn/Support for portal labels, diagnostic names, and expiration defaults.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The phrase "AI hacking" has become a fear-selling headline, and in selling it, it blurs &lt;strong&gt;three completely different threats&lt;/strong&gt;. Telling them apart is the difference between choosing your encryption well and buying smoke.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. Can an AI break the mathematics of encryption? No.
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is, today, no AI cryptanalysis that breaks well-designed primitives: AES, XChaCha20-Poly1305, ML-KEM or Argon2id. A neural network does not "guess" a 256-bit key nor speed up the algebra encryption rests on. The key space is so large that no amount of statistical compute walks it. &lt;strong&gt;Anyone claiming AI decrypts AES is selling fear, not describing an attack.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. What AI does amplify attacks the implementation and the human
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Real AI-assisted hacks do not touch the maths; they scale the three things that were always the weak link:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Implementation bugs.&lt;/strong&gt; AI reads code at scale and finds the human error: a reused nonce, a timing-leaking comparison, mishandled padding, a well-chosen primitive used badly. The cipher does not fail here — the program around it does.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Social engineering.&lt;/strong&gt; AI-generated phishing is more convincing and cheaper. Steal the master password and no cryptography protects a key handed over willingly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Guided brute force.&lt;/strong&gt; AI prioritises which passwords to try. The right defence already existed: a &lt;strong&gt;memory-hard&lt;/strong&gt; key derivation like Argon2id, tuned to hundreds of megabytes per attempt, costs &lt;em&gt;memory&lt;/em&gt;, not just compute — and memory does not parallelise cheaply on a GPU or a model. AI does not change that cost.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The lesson is old and still true: &lt;strong&gt;encryption is almost never broken; it is bypassed.&lt;/strong&gt; AI makes the bypass faster, not the wall weaker.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. The real threat to the mathematics is not AI: it is quantum computing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The only adversary aiming at the algebraic heart of encryption is quantum computing. Shor's algorithm breaks RSA and elliptic curve — the basis of almost all of today's asymmetric encryption. And the attack is not in the future: it is &lt;strong&gt;"harvest now, decrypt later."&lt;/strong&gt; An adversary captures your encrypted traffic today and stores it until the machine that opens it exists. Everything you encrypt today with classical cryptography has a silent expiry date.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The answer is not an "anti-AI" product. It is &lt;strong&gt;hybrid post-quantum cryptography&lt;/strong&gt;: combining the classical algorithm with a quantum-resistant one (for example X25519 with ML-KEM) so that breaking the message requires breaking &lt;strong&gt;both&lt;/strong&gt;. The hybrid protects even if one of the two turns out to have an undiscovered weakness.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How to choose encryption with this in mind
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If a vendor sells you their encryption as "AI-proof," be wary: they are describing a threat that does not exist and ignoring the one that does. The questions that actually discriminate are different:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Is it hybrid post-quantum?&lt;/strong&gt; — against "harvest now, decrypt later."&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Is the implementation audited?&lt;/strong&gt; — because the real vector is the code bug, and the best defence is to hunt it with the same tools the attacker will use, before they do.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Is the key derivation memory-hard?&lt;/strong&gt; — so a weak password does not fall to brute force at scale.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those three are measurable and honest. "Anti-AI" is none of the three.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week2-2026-10-12"&gt;Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 2: Side Hustle&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built &lt;strong&gt;Reelflow&lt;/strong&gt;, a content ideation tool that helps creators turn fresh stories in their niche into a rough Instagram Reel script.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The problem starts before someone presses record: finding something worth talking about, understanding the story, and turning it into a short video with a clear beginning and ending. For a solo creator or freelance social media manager, repeating that research for every Reel can take time away from actually creating.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reelflow brings those steps into one focused flow:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Choose a niche:&lt;/strong&gt; AI, technology, startups, coding, design, or space.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Find a spark:&lt;/strong&gt; explore cards containing fresh stories, source links, publication dates, and available community signals.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Get the context:&lt;/strong&gt; review the source highlights and choose a 30-, 45-, or 60-second Reel with a conversational, educational, or bold delivery.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Generate the flow:&lt;/strong&gt; get a &lt;strong&gt;hook, plot, twist, and conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;, with voiceover text, shot suggestions, and on-screen overlays.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Make it yours:&lt;/strong&gt; edit the voiceover, then copy or download the script with its source attribution.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is no login or signup. The interface moves through one stage at a time, using warm colors, modern typography, and animated transitions to make the process feel like a creative onboarding experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The potential paying customer is a creator or freelance social media manager who needs to research and outline niche content regularly. The value I want to sell is less time between finding a story and having a usable recording plan. Willingness to pay is still a hypothesis; I have not validated it with paying customers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Demo video:&lt;/strong&gt;   &lt;iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/ZShTy-lQwKE" width="710" height="399"&gt;
&lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The current version runs locally. It does not have a public deployment yet. To try it with Node.js 22+ and Ollama installed, run:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ollama pull qwen3:8b
npm start
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;Then open &lt;code&gt;http://localhost:3000&lt;/code&gt; with Ollama running.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A walkthrough can follow the complete journey: select Space &amp;amp; Discovery, choose a live article, review its summary, generate a 30-second Reel, edit a line, and download the finished draft.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During development, all six source feeds returned live stories. A real generation using Qwen3 8B produced all four script stages in approximately &lt;strong&gt;96 seconds on the development machine's CPU&lt;/strong&gt;. That is one observed run, not a guaranteed generation time. Browser visual verification was not completed in the development session.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


Code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GitHub repository:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="ltag-github-readme-tag"&gt;
&lt;div class="readme-overview"&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;
&lt;img src="https://assets.dev.to/assets/github-logo-5a155e1f9a670af7944dd5e12375bc76ed542ea80224905ecaf878b9157cdefc.svg" alt="GitHub logo"&gt;
&lt;a href="https://github.com/Bhavik-Pahwa" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;
Bhavik-Pahwa
&lt;/a&gt; / &lt;a href="https://github.com/Bhavik-Pahwa/Content-Ideation-Tool" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;
Content-Ideation-Tool
&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;

&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="ltag-github-body"&gt;

&lt;div id="readme" class="md"&gt;&lt;div class="markdown-heading"&gt;
&lt;h1 class="heading-element"&gt;Reelflow&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A local, no-login content studio that turns fresh niche stories into editable Instagram reel drafts. Six niches, live source cards, source highlights, and four scripted scenes: &lt;strong&gt;Hook → Plot → Twist → Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="markdown-heading"&gt;
&lt;h2 class="heading-element"&gt;Run&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Requirements: Node.js 22+ and &lt;a href="https://ollama.com/" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;Ollama&lt;/a&gt;. No npm dependencies or API keys are required.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight highlight-source-powershell notranslate position-relative overflow-auto js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;ollama pull qwen3:8b
npm start&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Open &lt;a href="http://localhost:3000" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;http://localhost:3000&lt;/a&gt;. Ollama must be running. On this machine, Qwen3 8B was already installed. A first request may take longer while the model loads. Scripts have a four-minute timeout, with a visible error and retry path. Run &lt;code&gt;npm test&lt;/code&gt; for server contract tests.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Optional PowerShell configuration:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight highlight-source-powershell notranslate position-relative overflow-auto js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;&lt;span class="pl-smi"&gt;$&lt;span class="pl-c1"&gt;env:&lt;/span&gt;OLLAMA_MODEL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pl-k"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pl-s"&gt;&lt;span class="pl-pds"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;qwen3:8b&lt;span class="pl-pds"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pl-smi"&gt;$&lt;span class="pl-c1"&gt;env:&lt;/span&gt;OLLAMA_URL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pl-k"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pl-s"&gt;&lt;span class="pl-pds"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;http://127.0.0.1:11434&lt;span class="pl-pds"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pl-smi"&gt;$&lt;span class="pl-c1"&gt;env:&lt;/span&gt;PORT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pl-k"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pl-s"&gt;&lt;span class="pl-pds"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;3000&lt;span class="pl-pds"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
npm start&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The app listens on loopback intentionally. It is a local MVP, not a multi-tenant production service. A public deployment needs an inference host, request quotas…&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="gh-btn-container"&gt;&lt;a class="gh-btn" href="https://github.com/Bhavik-Pahwa/Content-Ideation-Tool" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;View on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fo3fn7y5le4zh80b8xjpl.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fo3fn7y5le4zh80b8xjpl.png" alt=" " width="800" height="425"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fdrdpk2zox71nifiaoblo.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fdrdpk2zox71nifiaoblo.png" alt=" " width="800" height="425"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project includes an MIT license, setup instructions, API research notes, and five passing server tests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The main files are:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;server.mjs&lt;/code&gt;: source integrations, feed caching, Ollama requests, and generated-output validation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;public/app.js&lt;/code&gt;: the niche-to-script journey, editable voiceovers, and exports.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;public/style.css&lt;/code&gt;: responsive layouts, transitions, and reduced-motion support.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;server.test.mjs&lt;/code&gt;: server contracts, generation input checks, and malformed-output checks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;docs/API-RESEARCH.md&lt;/code&gt;: the niche-to-API mapping and source limitations.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Qwen3 8B, an open-weight model running locally through Ollama, is the creative engine.&lt;/strong&gt; Every generated script comes from the model; the app does not substitute a canned script when inference fails.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application uses a Node.js HTTP server and a plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript frontend. It has no npm runtime dependencies. The backend fetches stories from free public API endpoints and caches each niche's feed for five minutes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://hn.algolia.com/api" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Hacker News search through Algolia&lt;/a&gt; supplies recent AI, technology, and startup stories with points and comment counts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://developers.forem.com/api/v1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The DEV API&lt;/a&gt; supplies coding and design posts with descriptions and community reactions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://www.spaceflightnewsapi.net/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Spaceflight News API&lt;/a&gt; supplies recent space reporting with publisher summaries.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These signals need context: community activity is not proof that something is trending on Instagram, and recent space reporting is not a popularity ranking. Reelflow labels the distinction instead of inventing a viral score.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After a user selects a story, the server sends its available source context, desired duration, and delivery style to Ollama's &lt;code&gt;/api/chat&lt;/code&gt; endpoint. A JSON schema defines the four required scenes and their voiceover, visual, and overlay fields. The server validates the response again before displaying it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The prompt tells the model to treat source text as data, use only supplied facts, and avoid invented statistics or quotes. When a source supplies only a headline, the interface discloses that limitation and the prompt requests an exploratory angle. These safeguards do not guarantee factual accuracy: the creator still needs to check the original source before recording.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I used Codex to help implement and test the application. The generated Reel content itself is powered by the local Qwen model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open innovation makes the economics and ownership of this tool more flexible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Experimenting does not incur a paid inference API charge for each draft.&lt;/strong&gt; A creator can change the tone or regenerate an outline using their own machine. Hardware and electricity still cost money, and running a hosted service would add costs, but there is no per-generation model-provider bill in the local setup.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The writing process stays on the machine running Reelflow.&lt;/strong&gt; With the default local configuration, the selected story context and generated script are processed by Ollama on that computer. Discovering fresh stories still requires internet access, so this is not a fully offline research tool.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The creative engine can change with the product.&lt;/strong&gt; The model is configurable through &lt;code&gt;OLLAMA_MODEL&lt;/code&gt;, and the prompt and output schema are directly editable. I can adapt the workflow to a specific creator's niche or try another compatible local model without rebuilding around a different paid API contract. This version uses the existing Qwen3 model; I have not fine-tuned it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A closed API could also generate a script. The difference here is that I can run and inspect the application around a model I control locally, choose when to change it, and keep inference independent of a hosted provider's pricing. For a small tool aimed at repeat creative work, those choices matter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sometimes I need to see a problem before I can explain it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Who calls this service? What happens if the request fails? Is this actually one feature, or three separate problems hiding under the same name?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A few boxes and arrows can make those questions easier to answer. So can a mind map that starts messy and gradually becomes a plan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's why I still like UML—and why I rebuilt &lt;strong&gt;Codiva PlantUML&lt;/strong&gt;, my browser-based editor for text-based diagrams.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes, UML. The thing you may remember from university assignments, enormous class diagrams, and arguments about which arrowhead to use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I think there's still a lot to like once you bring it back to everyday development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://portafolio.codiva.cl/codiva-plantuml/" class="crayons-btn crayons-btn--primary" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Open Codiva PlantUML&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A little history: why all those boxes existed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before UML, there were several competing approaches to modeling object-oriented systems. Grady Booch, James Rumbaugh, and Ivar Jacobson brought their methods together, helping create the &lt;strong&gt;Unified Modeling Language&lt;/strong&gt;. It became an OMG standard in 1997.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The idea was useful: give people a shared visual vocabulary for describing software. A relationship or interaction shouldn't need an entirely new notation every time you change teams.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then came the baggage many developers associate with it: elaborate modeling tools, diagrams produced as deliverables, and designs that took longer to maintain than the code they described.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your introduction to UML involved modeling every getter and setter before writing a working program, I understand the lack of enthusiasm.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But I still find value in the smaller habit underneath it: &lt;strong&gt;put a model of the problem somewhere you can inspect it.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A sequence diagram can reveal a round trip nobody noticed. A state diagram can expose a transition nobody defined. Even an unfinished sketch can show that two people have been imagining different systems throughout the same meeting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those are useful discoveries before implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A diagram can help you think before it helps you document
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We often talk about diagrams as something you make after understanding a system, so someone else can understand it too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I like the earlier stage just as much: making a diagram &lt;em&gt;to find out what I understand&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Say you're planning an asynchronous report feature. In prose, it sounds simple:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The user requests a report, a worker generates it, and we notify them when it's ready.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start drawing it, and the questions appear:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Who owns the report's status?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What happens when the worker retries?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Can the user request the same report twice?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What does “ready” mean if notification fails?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The picture gives those questions somewhere to live. You can change the model while the change is still cheap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The diagram doesn't have to represent the entire system. Often the most useful one answers a single question.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Mind maps are a great place to start
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before I know the sequence of events, I may only know the pieces of the problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's where mind maps help: take a vague feature, give it a few branches, and keep splitting until the unknowns become visible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;PlantUML supports mind maps too.&lt;/strong&gt; They aren't part of the UML standard; PlantUML extends beyond UML into other useful diagram types.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's a small example you can paste into the editor:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;@startmindmap
* Asynchronous reports
** User experience
*** Request a report
*** Check progress
*** Download the result
** Processing
*** Queue the job
*** Generate the file
*** Retry failures
** Questions
*** Duplicate requests?
*** File expiration?
*** Notification failure?
@endmindmap
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The asterisks define the hierarchy. Add another level when an idea needs breaking down; move a branch when you realize it belongs elsewhere.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I find this useful for feature planning, exploring an unfamiliar domain, or organizing the questions I want to take into a technical discussion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An outline works too. Seeing its branches together can make gaps and uneven complexity easier to notice.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then, when one branch needs a closer look, you can turn it into a more specific diagram.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A few UML diagrams still earn their place
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You don't need to learn the whole notation to get something useful from it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;When I want to understand…&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;I reach for…&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Who talks to whom, and in what order&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A sequence diagram&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Which states exist and what changes them&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A state diagram&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;How a workflow branches&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;An activity diagram&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;How domain concepts relate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A class diagram&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;How the system's parts connect&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A component diagram&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A class diagram, for example, can show the relationships between an order, its items, and a payment without listing every method in the codebase.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A sequence diagram can make the difference between “request accepted” and “work completed” explicit:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;@startuml
actor User
participant API
queue Jobs
participant Worker

User -&amp;gt; API: Request report
API -&amp;gt; Jobs: Enqueue job
API --&amp;gt; User: Accepted + report ID
Jobs -&amp;gt; Worker: Deliver job
Worker -&amp;gt; Worker: Generate report

alt generation succeeds
Worker -&amp;gt; API: Mark report ready
else generation fails
Worker -&amp;gt; API: Record failure
end
@enduml
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This sketch also leaves things unresolved. How does the user learn that the report is ready? What happens after a failure?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's a useful next conversation. The diagram has already helped identify it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why I like writing diagrams as text
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Visual editors are great for sketching freely. With PlantUML, I like being able to describe the relationships and let the renderer handle their arrangement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That gives me a few practical advantages:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Reviewable changes.&lt;/strong&gt; A pull request can show a new participant, message, or state transition.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Source beside the code.&lt;/strong&gt; A &lt;code&gt;.puml&lt;/code&gt; file can follow the same branches and review process as the feature it explains.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Easy reuse.&lt;/strong&gt; Copy a flow, adjust a few lines, and explore an alternative.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Less time arranging boxes.&lt;/strong&gt; I can focus on the interaction before worrying about presentation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are trade-offs. Automatic layout won't always look the way you want, and a large diagram can become difficult to read regardless of how you generate it. Text also doesn't keep documentation accurate by itself; someone still has to maintain it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For me, the sweet spot is a small, editable diagram with a clear purpose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you already use Mermaid and it fits your workflow, great. I see the habit of keeping useful diagrams as text as the bigger win. PlantUML happens to be the tool I wanted to build around.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Meet Codiva PlantUML
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I had an older editor built with CodeMirror 5, Bootstrap, and CDN dependencies. It rendered through the public PlantUML server and had been sitting there for a while.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I rebuilt it with &lt;strong&gt;Astro, React islands, CodeMirror 6, and my own containerized PlantUML engine&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal was a convenient place to open an example, work through an idea, and export a diagram without setting up a local environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The editor includes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Syntax highlighting and autocomplete for participants you've declared.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Syntax diagnostics with line highlighting.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Formatting from the context menu.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dark mode.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;SVG and PNG export.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Six examples to start from.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No account required.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rendering happens on my backend, so diagram source is sent to that server when you use the hosted editor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's free to use. There's an optional coffee link in the footer to help with the server bill.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A couple of things I learned rebuilding it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Running the engine myself gave me more control over responsiveness and diagnostics. It also gave me a few bugs worth remembering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Keep the JVM warm
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Launching a new JVM for each render took around &lt;strong&gt;1,115 ms&lt;/strong&gt; in my setup. Keeping PlantUML running brought the warm local engine measurement down to around &lt;strong&gt;14 ms&lt;/strong&gt;, averaged over 10 requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Over HTTPS, I observed roughly &lt;strong&gt;180 ms&lt;/strong&gt;, compared with around &lt;strong&gt;560 ms&lt;/strong&gt; for the public endpoint in my comparison. Those are observations from my deployment, rather than a general benchmark; network location, diagram complexity, and server load all matter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;PlantUML's built-in &lt;a href="https://plantuml.com/picoweb" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PicoWeb server&lt;/a&gt; provides a simple way to keep the engine running:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;java &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-jar&lt;/span&gt; plantuml.jar &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-picoweb&lt;/span&gt;:8777
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


A successful response can contain an unsuccessful diagram
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My sequence-diagram test passed. A class diagram then failed because the container was missing Graphviz.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The surprising part: the response was &lt;strong&gt;HTTP 200 with a valid SVG containing the error message&lt;/strong&gt;. My status-code check was happy. The person trying to render the diagram wasn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I used &lt;strong&gt;Smetana&lt;/strong&gt;, PlantUML's Java port of Graphviz, to handle layout in my setup. The editor also needed useful diagnostics rather than leaving users to read an error drawn inside an image.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the improvement I care about most: if you make a syntax mistake while thinking through a flow, the editor should help you get back to the flow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
Implementation detail: selecting Smetana and mapping error lines
&lt;p&gt;PlantUML supports selecting Smetana in diagram source:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;!pragma layout smetana
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Its documentation also lists the CLI option &lt;code&gt;-Playout=smetana&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In my server integration, the proxy injects the pragma into the rendering copy, leaving the editor contents and exported source as written. That added line means diagnostic positions need mapping back to the original source.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My application layer uses PlantUML diagnostics to return a 422 response with a line number for syntax errors. That response contract is part of the editor's backend; starting PicoWeb alone doesn't provide it.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;br&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Give one of your ideas a diagram
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you've been carrying a feature around in your head, try putting its main branches into a mind map. If a flow keeps getting lost in paragraphs, try a sequence diagram.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the kind of everyday use I built this editor for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://portafolio.codiva.cl/codiva-plantuml/" class="crayons-btn crayons-btn--primary" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Try Codiva PlantUML — no account needed&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd love feedback on the editor, especially anything that gets in the way of turning an idea into a readable diagram.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Do you still use UML—or did diagrams find their way back into your workflow through mind maps, Mermaid, or something else?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


References and further reading
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.omg.org/uml/why-uml-is-important.htm" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OMG: UML's origins and purpose&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://plantuml.com/mindmap-diagram" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PlantUML: mind map syntax&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://plantuml.com/layout-engines" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PlantUML: layout engines&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://plantuml.com/picoweb" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PlantUML: PicoWeb server&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every .NET codebase I have worked on hits the same wall sooner or later. The test suite grows, CI goes from three minutes to twenty, and every pull request pays the full price, even a one-line rename deep inside a helper class.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Splitting the suite or buying bigger runners helps for a while. But most of those tests could never have noticed the change in the first place. They run anyway because nothing tells CI which ones matter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I built &lt;strong&gt;TestDetta&lt;/strong&gt;: a .NET tool and GitHub Action that reads your diff and runs only the test classes that can observe it. It follows one rule throughout: &lt;strong&gt;when it cannot be sure, it runs more tests, never fewer.&lt;/strong&gt; A missed test is a bug. An extra test only costs CI time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This post covers how it works, how to try it in a few minutes, and how to check that it holds up on your own code before you trust it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A real example
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a real change to &lt;a href="https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-dotnet" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OpenTelemetry .NET&lt;/a&gt;: a local variable renamed inside one method. It changes no behaviour, but a normal pipeline still runs all 30 test projects and 274 test classes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TestDetta works it out like this:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Projects:&lt;/strong&gt; 2 of 30 test projects are affected. The other 28 are not even built.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Symbols:&lt;/strong&gt; 1 member changed, &lt;code&gt;WarnIfConfigFileEnvVarDiffers&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Coverage map:&lt;/strong&gt; 8 test classes ran that method on &lt;code&gt;main&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Result:&lt;/strong&gt; 8 of 274 classes run, 266 are skipped.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those 8 classes are exactly the ones that fail when that method is broken. Recorded test time went from &lt;strong&gt;5 m 59 s to 22.4 s&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it in two minutes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TestDetta is a .NET tool (it runs on the .NET 10 SDK; your projects can target older frameworks):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;dotnet tool &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--global&lt;/span&gt; TestDetta
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Ask what your branch affects, and why:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;td affected &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--base&lt;/span&gt; origin/main
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;See the &lt;code&gt;dotnet test&lt;/code&gt; commands it would run, without running anything:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;td &lt;span class="nb"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--base&lt;/span&gt; origin/main &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--dry-run&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And run only the affected tests:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;td &lt;span class="nb"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--base&lt;/span&gt; origin/main
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The output looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ td test --base origin/main
TestDetta 0.2.2 · 1 changed file · 2 affected test projects
Tests to run
┌──────────────────┬──────────┬─────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Test project     │ Run      │ Classes │ Why                                                      │
├──────────────────┼──────────┼─────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ tests/Shop.Tests │ filtered │  3 / 41 │ 3 of 41 test class(es) run or reference the changed code │
│ tests/Api.Tests  │ skipped  │  0 / 12 │ no test class runs or references the changed code        │
└──────────────────┴──────────┴─────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
tests/Shop.Tests → 3 classes
Shop.Tests.CartTests
Shop.Tests.CheckoutTests
Shop.Tests.OrderTests
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Without a coverage map, TestDetta decides by tracing names through your source. For sharper selection, record a map once on your default branch:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;td record
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This runs every test with coverage and writes one map per test project to &lt;code&gt;.testdetta/coverage&lt;/code&gt; (add &lt;code&gt;.testdetta/&lt;/code&gt; to your &lt;code&gt;.gitignore&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Need machine-readable output? &lt;code&gt;td affected --format json&lt;/code&gt; gives you the selection for your own scripts, &lt;code&gt;--format paths&lt;/code&gt; lists the test projects, and &lt;code&gt;--format commands&lt;/code&gt; prints the &lt;code&gt;dotnet test&lt;/code&gt; commands.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How it decides
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each step narrows the selection only when it has evidence. Otherwise it falls back to something wider.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Projects.&lt;/strong&gt; Changed files are mapped to their projects and followed through &lt;code&gt;ProjectReference&lt;/code&gt;s. Build-wide files such as &lt;code&gt;Directory.Build.props&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;global.json&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;.editorconfig&lt;/code&gt; affect every project below them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Symbols.&lt;/strong&gt; For C#, Visual Basic and F# files, the old and new syntax trees are compared (Roslyn for C# and VB). Comment and whitespace edits select nothing. Otherwise TestDetta knows exactly which types and members changed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Coverage.&lt;/strong&gt; A map of which test class ran which method, recorded at the base commit, gives the test classes for changed methods, properties and constructors directly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Names.&lt;/strong&gt; What coverage cannot see, such as new code, fields and constants, is traced through the source by name until it reaches test classes or code the map knows.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fallbacks.&lt;/strong&gt; When a change cannot be traced, the whole test project runs. That covers non-code files the build uses (&lt;code&gt;appsettings.json&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;.csproj&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;.resx&lt;/code&gt;), global usings, assembly attributes, and types a framework discovers through reflection (controllers, message handlers, EF configurations).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is one more safety net: if a filter matches no test at all, TestDetta treats that as its own bug and runs the project again without the filter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A few details took a lot of work to get right:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Maps don't have to be fresh.&lt;/strong&gt; The best map is one recorded at your branch's merge base. A map up to 50 commits older still works: TestDetta also selects the classes that ran anything changed in between that could reach your change.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Source generators are handled.&lt;/strong&gt; Generated code is read from the PDBs during recording. When a change reaches a generator in your repository, TestDetta builds the test projects that use it and compares what is generated, framework by framework.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;#if&lt;/code&gt; code is not missed.&lt;/strong&gt; C# files are parsed with each target framework's real preprocessor symbols, and with every symbol their &lt;code&gt;#if&lt;/code&gt;s name.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Your test configuration is respected.&lt;/strong&gt; Your own &lt;code&gt;--filter&lt;/code&gt;, run settings and runner arguments are combined with TestDetta's class filter, so it only ever narrows your runs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


In GitHub Actions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The usual setup records a map on every push to &lt;code&gt;main&lt;/code&gt; and runs only the affected tests on pull requests:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;push&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;branches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;pull_request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="na"&gt;permissions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;contents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;read&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;actions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;read&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# lets pull requests find the newest coverage map in the Actions cache&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="na"&gt;jobs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;runs-on&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;ubuntu-latest&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;steps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;uses&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;actions/checkout@v7&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# pin to a commit SHA&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;fetch-depth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# TestDetta compares with the merge base&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;persist-credentials&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;uses&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;actions/setup-dotnet@v6&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# pin to a commit SHA&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;global-json-file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;global.json&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Full test run of main, which also records the coverage map&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;github.event_name == 'push'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;uses&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;TestDetta/action@a2f55a8252840a367f2f7a9ebd6733de3ebfb2f5&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# v0.2.2&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;mode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;record&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Pull requests run only what the change can affect&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;github.event_name == 'pull_request'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;uses&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;TestDetta/action@a2f55a8252840a367f2f7a9ebd6733de3ebfb2f5&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# v0.2.2&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;mode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;dotnet-test-args&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;--logger trx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Maps live in the Actions cache, keyed by commit, and pull requests read the map of their merge base. Every run writes a job summary explaining what ran and why.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not on GitHub? GitLab CI, Azure DevOps, Jenkins and Bitbucket use the same two commands (&lt;code&gt;td record&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;td test&lt;/code&gt;) with a folder of per-commit maps on a shared disk or object storage (&lt;code&gt;--maps-dir&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Don't trust it, check it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A tool that decides which tests to skip has to earn your trust on your own codebase, not on a marketing page. TestDetta ships two ways to check it, and neither needs a license:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Shadow mode&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;td test --shadow&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;mode: shadow&lt;/code&gt; in the action) keeps running your full suite and compares the result with what the selection would have run. When a test fails outside the selection, it reruns that class to tell a real miss from a flaky or pre-existing failure. Run it for a couple of weeks of normal pull requests and you have evidence from your own code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Audit&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;td audit&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;mode: audit&lt;/code&gt;) doesn't wait for real failures. It breaks your code on purpose, one member at a time with IL mutants in a copy of the working tree, runs every test that can see each change, and reports any failing class the selection left out. It also reports the 95% upper bound of the miss rate. Your sources and build output are only read.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How it is tested
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I run the same check at scale on &lt;strong&gt;53 open-source .NET repositories&lt;/strong&gt;, including Polly, Newtonsoft.Json, AutoMapper, Serilog, Markdig, Refit, Autofac, NodaTime, FastEndpoints, FluentValidation and OpenTelemetry. Members are changed one at a time, and every test class that fails must be one TestDetta selected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From the latest round (2026-10-13 to 2026-10-15):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;3,071&lt;/strong&gt; changes broke at least one test.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;5&lt;/strong&gt; misses, each a documented limitation (more on those below). Earlier real misses were fixed, each with a regression test that fails without the fix.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The median repository ran &lt;strong&gt;35%&lt;/strong&gt; of its test classes per change. In 20 of the 53 repositories it was a quarter or less, and Polly got down to 3%.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To be upfront: some repositories gain much less. Mapperly ran 97% and AutoMapper 86%, because nearly every test there goes through the same central code paths. If most of your tests touch everything, test impact analysis can't save much, and the evaluation shows that honestly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Limitations
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TestDetta is static and coverage-based analysis, not magic. Here is what it can't fully see:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Code that runs once per process&lt;/strong&gt; (static constructors, lazy singletons, EF model building) is credited to the first test class that triggers it. TestDetta traces changes to such code by name instead, but a cache you wrote by hand (a &lt;code&gt;Dictionary&lt;/code&gt; checked with &lt;code&gt;TryGetValue&lt;/code&gt;) can still hide its users.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Reflection and dynamic loading&lt;/strong&gt; are only partly visible. Types a framework discovers this way run their whole test project.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;F# signature files&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;.fsi&lt;/code&gt;) are not analysed, so a change to one runs its project's dependents.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tests skipped while recording&lt;/strong&gt; have no coverage, for example a missing Docker daemon. Record where your tests actually run.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The full list is on the &lt;a href="https://testdetta.com/docs/limitations/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Limitations page&lt;/a&gt;. My recommendation: keep running the full suite on your default branch. Recording does that anyway, so anything a selection could ever hide is still caught before release.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What's supported
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Test frameworks:&lt;/strong&gt; xUnit v2, xUnit v3, NUnit, MSTest and TUnit&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Runners:&lt;/strong&gt; VSTest and Microsoft.Testing.Platform (read from &lt;code&gt;global.json&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Languages:&lt;/strong&gt; C#, Visual Basic and F#&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CI:&lt;/strong&gt; GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Azure DevOps, Jenkins, Bitbucket and on-premises setups&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Privacy and licensing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of your code leaves your CI. Analysis, coverage maps and test results stay on your machines. The only network call is an optional license renewal, at most once a day, that sends the license and nothing else.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TestDetta is &lt;strong&gt;free for public repositories on GitHub Actions and GitLab CI&lt;/strong&gt;, with every feature and no sign-up. It detects public repositories from the CI itself. Private repositories need a paid license, with a 30-day free trial that needs no card. A license problem never fails your build: without a valid license, every test simply runs, as it did before TestDetta.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Links
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Website and docs: &lt;a href="https://testdetta.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;testdetta.com&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How it decides, step by step: &lt;a href="https://testdetta.com/docs/how-it-works/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;testdetta.com/docs/how-it-works&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Validate it on your repository: &lt;a href="https://testdetta.com/docs/validate/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;testdetta.com/docs/validate&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;GitHub Action: &lt;a href="https://github.com/TestDetta/action" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/TestDetta/action&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;NuGet: &lt;a href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/TestDetta" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;nuget.org/packages/TestDetta&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you try it, I would love to hear how it goes, especially if it &lt;strong&gt;misses&lt;/strong&gt; a test. A miss is the one bug I care about most. Leave a comment here or write to &lt;a href="mailto:support@testdetta.com"&gt;support@testdetta.com&lt;/a&gt;, and I'll dig in.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How does your team deal with a slow .NET test suite today? I'm curious what has worked for you.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A single AI visibility score can hide the answer marketers most need: whether a brand appears when a prospective buyer asks a specific AI assistant for help. Fractl's research into generative engine optimization finds that brand recall differs substantially by model, category and prompt intent. For businesses trying to &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/ai-brand-mentions-citations-semrush-visibility-index/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;earn mentions in ChatGPT, Gemini or Claude&lt;/a&gt;, the practical implication is clear: visibility should be measured separately, not blended into one headline number.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fractl analyzed 8,090 keywords across 25 verticals and 22,410 leading domains. Its dataset covered 4,320 responses from GPT-4o in ChatGPT, Gemini 2.5 Flash and Claude Sonnet 4.6, using prompts intended to reflect buyer behavior in each category. The results, detailed in &lt;a href="https://www.frac.tl/generative-engine-optimization-brand-visibility-ai-search/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Fractl's Generative Engine Optimization study&lt;/a&gt;, show why a conventional ranking-style dashboard is an incomplete way to evaluate AI discovery.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Only &lt;strong&gt;11% of brands&lt;/strong&gt; in the dataset were referenced by all three models. About &lt;strong&gt;77% were mentioned by just one&lt;/strong&gt;. That means a brand can look visible in one assistant while being absent from another, even when users ask closely related questions. A blended score may smooth over that gap, but it cannot tell a marketing team where visibility is actually being won or lost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why AI brand recall must be measured by model and intent
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The central finding is not that one model is universally better at naming brands. It is that each model can produce a different set of recommendations, and those differences change with the category and question. A travel prompt, an insurance question and a lifestyle recommendation do not create the same competitive field. Fractl observed concentrated travel results, a greater presence for digital-native insurance players, and direct-to-consumer brands appearing prominently in lifestyle-related results.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That makes category context as important as the model itself. A business that sells into more than one use case should not test one broad question and treat the result as representative. It should assess the buyer questions that matter to its own products, services and market position.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Brand visibility pattern in Fractl's dataset&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Share of brands&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What it means for an audit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Referenced by GPT-4o, Gemini 2.5 Flash and Claude Sonnet 4.6&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;11%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Broad recall across the three tested models, but still dependent on the tested prompts and categories.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Referenced by only one of the three models&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;About 77%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A single-model result can overstate a brand's reach across AI assistants.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fractl also identified a &lt;strong&gt;default brands effect&lt;/strong&gt;. Some brands surfaced repeatedly across prompts and categories, even when they were not the leaders in traditional SEO metrics. At the same time, conventional search authority generally correlated with AI recall for most brands. The relationship was not absolute: roughly 5% of brands were underexposed in large language models relative to their search standing, while about 4% were AI overperformers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For marketers, this is a useful distinction. Strong organic search performance remains relevant, but it is not a guarantee that an AI assistant will mention a brand. Nor does weak conventional visibility necessarily rule out strong AI recall in a particular category.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


A practical model-by-model audit
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A useful audit starts with &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/google-ai-mode-buyer-queries-seo-content-gaps/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the questions buyers are likely to ask&lt;/a&gt;, rather than a generic prompt such as "best companies in [category]." Group prompts by category and intent. For example, separate research-oriented questions, comparison questions and recommendation requests where those reflect real customer journeys.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then document which brands each model names, how often they recur across the prompt set, and which sources the models cite where citations are provided. Fractl's GEO framework recommends looking at these cited sources because they can indicate the types of coverage associated with credibility in an AI-generated answer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A practical workflow should include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Map model coverage&lt;/strong&gt; by recording whether ChatGPT, Gemini and Claude mention the brand for each relevant prompt.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Segment the prompt set&lt;/strong&gt; by product category and buyer intent, so results do not collapse unrelated questions into one score.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/ai-citations-brand-sentiment-study/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Review cited sources&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; to identify publications, pages or third-party references that appear alongside AI answers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Compare recurring competitors&lt;/strong&gt; to see which brands have become defaults for the category.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Repeat the audit over time&lt;/strong&gt;, because model versions, underlying data and prompt behavior can change results.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This approach does not turn AI recall into a fully controllable metric. It does make the gap between a brand's desired category position and its current AI presence visible enough to act on.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Improve category signals beyond owned content
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fractl argues that improving recall depends less on simply publishing more generic content and more on reinforcing clear category associations across the wider web. Owned pages remain part of that work, especially when they explain what a company offers in precise, useful language. But the study's framework places substantial weight on &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/ai-search-business-review-replies-third-party-coverage/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;third-party coverage and digital PR&lt;/a&gt; that support the same association through high-authority publishers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In practice, that means teams should connect content work with earned-media efforts. If a business wants to be associated with a defined product category, its website, expert commentary, research, partnerships and relevant external coverage should consistently communicate that positioning. The goal is not to chase mentions indiscriminately. It is to build credible, category-relevant signals that make the association easier to recognize across the web ecosystem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The study's scope also sets important limits. It tested three AI models and a fixed prompt set, so its percentages should not be treated as a permanent market-wide benchmark. Results may change as models, their underlying data and the ways people phrase queries evolve. The durable lesson is methodological: measure the models and questions that matter to the business, then avoid treating one aggregated score as the whole story.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For businesses that rely on search discovery, this shift affects everyday marketing workflows. SEO reporting can remain a useful baseline, but it should sit alongside a recurring AI visibility review that tracks category prompts, competitor recall and external sources. That provides a more specific basis for deciding whether to strengthen a product page, develop a research asset, pursue relevant coverage or refine the way a category is described.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Businesses that cannot see how AI assistants describe their category risk spending on content without knowing whether it changes discovery. GEO Search Leads helps teams turn model-by-model findings into a practical plan for stronger &lt;a href="https://geosearchleads.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AI search visibility and qualified lead opportunities&lt;/a&gt;, connecting category positioning, cited-source analysis and measurable follow-up work. Start an AI visibility audit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Frequently Asked Questions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What does AI visibility mean for a brand?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI visibility is the extent to which an AI assistant mentions or recommends a brand in response to relevant user prompts. Fractl's research indicates that this visibility varies by model, category and prompt intent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why is one AI visibility score misleading?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fractl found that only 11% of brands in its dataset were referenced by all three tested models, while about 77% were mentioned by only one. A combined score can conceal major differences between assistants.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Which AI models did Fractl study?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The study analyzed responses from GPT-4o in ChatGPT, Gemini 2.5 Flash and Claude Sonnet 4.6.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How can a business improve its AI brand recall?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fractl recommends auditing relevant prompts by model and intent, reviewing cited sources, and strengthening category associations through owned content, third-party coverage and digital PR.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Conclusion
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fractl's findings make the case for treating AI visibility as a set of model-specific, category-specific signals rather than a single marketing metric. Brands that audit the buyer questions relevant to their market, identify gaps across assistants and build credible category signals across the web will have a more useful foundation for AI search strategy than those relying on one blended score.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Automating Multi‑Platform Content Publishing: Fixing Bluesky JSON Validation and Keeping Metadata in Sync
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; I updated the content‑automation pipeline to generate a schema‑compliant Bluesky JSON payload and synchronized the &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; flags after each successful publish. The changes eliminate runtime validation errors and give us a reliable source‑of‑truth for what has been generated.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our weekly “content‑automation” job creates markdown articles for Medium and Substack, then builds platform‑specific payloads for Bluesky. The last run (2026‑10‑14) failed with the following error from the Bluesky validation endpoint:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight http"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;POST https://api.bsky.app/xrpc/com.atproto.repo.createRecord
Response 400: {"error":"InvalidRequest","message":"JSON schema validation failed: required property 'text' missing"}
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The script was reading &lt;code&gt;content/2026/10/13/VS/bluesky_es.json&lt;/code&gt;, but the generated file was missing the required &lt;code&gt;text&lt;/code&gt; field and contained an extra &lt;code&gt;type&lt;/code&gt; value that the API rejects. At the same time, &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; still reported &lt;code&gt;"medium_generated": false&lt;/code&gt; even though the Medium markdown files had been written, causing our CI checks to think the publish step was incomplete.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


What I Tried First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first attempt was to patch the JSON after it was written, adding a &lt;code&gt;text&lt;/code&gt; property manually:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// scripts/generate_bluesky_payload.js (initial version)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;srcPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;utf8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// quick fix&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;destPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I also added a post‑publish step that toggled the flags in &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// scripts/update_metadata.js (initial version)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;content/2026/10/14/content-automation/metadata.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;medium_generated&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Both patches compiled, but the API still rejected the payload because the &lt;code&gt;type&lt;/code&gt; field in the JSON array (&lt;code&gt;"avance"&lt;/code&gt; for Spanish, &lt;code&gt;"progress"&lt;/code&gt; for English) is not part of the allowed schema. Moreover, the manual &lt;code&gt;text&lt;/code&gt; injection left the content empty, resulting in blank posts on Bluesky.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Refactor the Bluesky payload generator
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The generator now builds the payload from a &lt;strong&gt;typed model&lt;/strong&gt; instead of copying raw JSON. I introduced &lt;code&gt;src/models/blueskyPost.js&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/models/blueskyPost.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BlueskyPost&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/**
* @param {string} language  'en' | 'es'
* @param {string} headline  Title of the post
* @param {string} body      Full markdown content
*/&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;constructor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;language&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;headline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;$type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;app.bsky.feed.post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;language&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;headline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;\n\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;createdAt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toISOString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toJSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Only keep properties required by the API&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;$type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;createdAt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;$type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;createdAt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;exports&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;BlueskyPost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The generator script (&lt;code&gt;scripts/generate_bluesky_payload.js&lt;/code&gt;) now imports this model:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// scripts/generate_bluesky_payload.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;BlueskyPost&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;../src/models/blueskyPost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Helper to read markdown source&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;readMarkdown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;locale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;mdPath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;__dirname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`../content/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/VS/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;locale&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;en&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;medium_en.md&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;medium_es.md&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;mdPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;utf8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Build payload for both locales&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;buildPayload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;enBody&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;readMarkdown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;en&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;esBody&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;readMarkdown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;es&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;enPost&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BlueskyPost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;en&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Weekly Build Log – Receipts, Keep‑Alive, and Automation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;enBody&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;esPost&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BlueskyPost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;es&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Esta semana en content‑automation – keep‑alive, recibos y automatización&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;esBody&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;enPost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toJSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;esPost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toJSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()];&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Write JSON files that match the API schema&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;writePayload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;buildPayload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;outDir&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;__dirname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`../content/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/VS`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;mkdirSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;outDir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;recursive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// English payload&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;outDir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;bluesky_en.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Spanish payload&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;outDir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;bluesky_es.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Run for the target date&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;targetDate&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2026/10/13&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writePayload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;targetDate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;✅ Bluesky payload generated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Key changes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Explicit schema&lt;/strong&gt; – The &lt;code&gt;BlueskyPost&lt;/code&gt; class guarantees &lt;code&gt;$type&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;lang&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;text&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;createdAt&lt;/code&gt; are present.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No stray &lt;code&gt;type&lt;/code&gt; field&lt;/strong&gt; – The previous &lt;code&gt;type: "avance"&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;"progress"&lt;/code&gt; entries were removed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Single source of truth&lt;/strong&gt; – Both English and Spanish posts pull the same markdown files used for Medium/Substack, preventing drift.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Update the Git diff for the new JSON files
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The commit that added the corrected payloads (&lt;code&gt;c15cdc88&lt;/code&gt;) now contains clean JSON:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;content/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;14&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;/content-automation/bluesky_en.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"$type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"app.bsky.feed.post"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"lang"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"en"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"text"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Weekly Build Log – Receipts, Keep‑Alive, and Automation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\n\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;I woke up on a Tuesday morning ..."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"createdAt"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2026-10-14T07:05:12.000Z"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;content/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;14&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;/content-automation/bluesky_es.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"$type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"app.bsky.feed.post"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"lang"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"es"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"text"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Esta semana en content‑automation – keep‑alive, recibos y automatización&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\n\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Cuando me desperté ..."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"createdAt"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2026-10-14T07:05:12.000Z"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

